body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # Aeonic Standalone Civil Clock v1 Snapshot 0.1 [# civil-clock-working-contract]

  Status: immutable compatibility snapshot
  [$ aeonic-temporal-civil-clock-specs-v1-snapshot-0.1], frozen 2026-11-18.
  Proposal lifecycle and disabled publication are preserved. Implementation
  conformance and native capacity require separate evidence. Earlier profiles,
  defaults and snapshots retain their identities and bytes.

  Implementations MUST satisfy the closed
  [@ /artifacts/assets/aeonic-operations/v1/snapshots/0.1/civil-clock-contract.schema.json | operation schema]
  and the semantics, resource guards and deterministic diagnostics below. Schema
  validity alone is insufficient. The matching CTS snapshot is
  [$ aeonic-temporal-civil-clock-cts-v1-snapshot-0.1].

  ## 1. Domain and capabilities [# civil-clock-section-1]

  The profile identifier is [$ aeonic.operations.temporal.civil-clock.v1].
  The capability identifiers are:

  - [$ Aeonic.Operations.Temporal.CivilClock.Relations]: [$ equal], [$ compare], [$ relate].
  - [$ Aeonic.Operations.Temporal.CivilClock.Arithmetic]: [$ shiftClock], [$ step], [$ materialize].

  Each claimed capability is complete, without case opt-outs. Availability is trusted
  host configuration, separate from a requested claim or logical operation. A
  relations-only provider accepts derived sets without implementing a producer.
  Arithmetic accepts specified numerical bundles without claiming numerical arithmetic.

  Coordinates lie on one calculation-relative axis. Day [$ 0] is the conventional
  origin for every authored time admitted to this profile. A coordinate denotes
  [$ 86,400 × relativeDay + clockSeconds], exactly. Days are signed integers, with
  capacity limits rather than calendar-year limits. Hours are 00–23 and ordinary
  minutes/seconds 00–59. This defines civil-coordinate movement and ordering, not
  elapsed time or the order of real dated events. It does not assign a date, host
  timezone, occurrence identity, UTC realization, leap rule or external reference.

  Midnight has an ordinary coordinate on the next relative day. There is no special
  end-of-day/domain marker, infinity, [$ 24:00] member or wrap performed as a side effect.
  Different relative days remain different values under equality. Clock-face
  projection, wrapping and date anchoring are outside this first contract.

  ## 2. Closed reusable values [# civil-clock-section-2]

  ### Authored or materialized clock [# civil-clock-section-3]

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "temporal": { "category": "civilClock", "value": "23:59" },
    "originClockForm": "minute",
    "history": "none-declared"
  }
  ~~~$

  Only [$ temporal] is required on authored input. Infer omitted origin from the
  literal ([$ hour], [$ minute], [$ second]); default omitted history to [$ none-declared].
  Retain a supplied valid origin independently of the literal's current shape.
  Origins may also be [$ undeclared]; history may be [$ approximation-declared].
  No supplied metadata may be null or an alias for omission.

  Admitted spellings are exactly [$ HH:], [$ HH:MM], or [$ HH:MM:SS] with optional [$ .]
  and one or more ASCII digits after seconds. All fields have fixed widths and
  intrinsic ordinary clock ranges. Do not accept whitespace, [$ HH] alone, [$ T],
  dates, offsets, [$ Z], [$ &], decimal commas, [$ 24:00] or second [$ 60] in this category.
  These admission rules do not change Core's broader preservation grammar.

  An hour/minute covers its complete half-open interval on reference day [$ 0].
  Explicit seconds identify one exact point; an omitted fraction is zero.
  Fractions are exact coordinates, not measurement bins. Preserve authored text,
  including fractional trailing zeroes, at the operand boundary. A produced
  materialization uses the canonical fraction spelling, not the original scale.

  ### Derived exact set [# civil-clock-section-4]

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "temporal": {
      "category": "civilClockSet",
      "pieces": [
        {
          "kind": "interval",
          "start": { "day": "0", "time": "23:59:00.5" },
          "end": { "day": "1", "time": "00:00:00.5" },
          "includeStart": true,
          "includeEnd": false
        }
      ]
    },
    "originClockForm": "minute",
    "history": "none-declared"
  }
  ~~~$

  All three bundle fields are required. The descriptor has exactly [$ category] and
  [$ pieces]. The array is nonempty. A point has exactly [$ kind: "point"] and
  [$ at: {day, time}]. An interval has exactly the fields in the example and requires
  strict start < end; every zero-width interval is rejected, including a closed
  one. Endpoint flags are JSON Booleans expressing membership, not predicate results.

  Every coordinate object has exactly [$ day] and [$ time]. Day is canonical integer
  text: [$ 0], a nonzero unsigned decimal integer, or minus followed by a nonzero
  decimal integer. No leading plus, leading zeroes, [$ -0], exponent, decimal point
  or host numerical payload. Time is full [$ HH:MM:SS], optionally followed by a
  nonzero fraction whose last digit is nonzero. Omit an all-zero fraction and trim
  trailing fractional zeroes in produced coordinates. Incoming derived coordinates
  MUST already satisfy that form. Clock fields keep their fixed-width leading zeroes.

  Order coordinates by the numerical signed day first, then exact clock value.
  Use virtual zero padding when comparing fractions; do not expand a common grid
  or sort day strings lexically. In a negative result use floor division:
  [$ -0.5] coordinate seconds is [$ {day:"-1",time:"23:59:59.5"}]. The time field
  always stays within one ordinary day. There is no negative zero coordinate.

  ### Numerical amount [# civil-clock-section-5]

  [$ shiftClock] reuses the existing closed numerical value/history bundles:

  ~~~$ json
  { "number": { "category": "finiteNumber", "value": "0.5" }, "history": "none-declared" }
  ~~~$

  ~~~$ json
  { "number": { "category": "exactNumber", "coefficient": "5", "exponent": "-1" }, "history": "approximation-declared" }
  ~~~$

  Finite Number text uses the inherited grammar:

  ~~~$ text
  [+-]?(?:(?:0|[1-9][0-9]*)(?:\\.[0-9]+)?|\\.[0-9]+)(?:[eE][+-]?[0-9]+)?
  ~~~$

  The grammar is anchored to the complete string. Preserve authored spelling. In normalized
  derived [$ c × 10^e], coefficient has no leading/trailing zeroes except [$ 0],
  whose exponent MUST be [$ 0]. Exponent is canonical signed integer text.
  Finite input may omit history; exact-decimal input requires it. A rational
  descriptor is a recognized deferred category, not an implicitly rounded amount.
  Directly use a numerical operation's returned [$ value] bundle; its entire response
  envelope is not an operand. No fraction literal or duration syntax is introduced.

  ## 3. Exact canonical unions [# civil-clock-section-6]

  Normalize internally produced candidate pieces by ascending lower coordinate,
  included lower before excluded, descending upper coordinate, included upper
  before excluded. Internally treat a point as equal included bounds. Scan this
  order and merge overlaps, or touching pieces when at least one touching endpoint
  includes the shared coordinate. On coincident outer bounds use the union of
  membership. Emit singletons as dedicated points and positive-width components
  as intervals. Remove covered points. Never fill a gap or missing shared point.

  Incoming derived sets MUST already be sorted and canonical: no overlaps,
  redundant points or mergeable neighbors. Reject a zero/reversed interval at its
  [$ end]; reject an offending cross-piece order/mergeability at that piece's [$ at]
  or [$ start]. Validate its fields/coordinates/flags before cross-piece checks.
  No consumer silently sorts or repairs input. A continuous interval may cross
  arbitrarily many relative days within admitted coordinate capacity, without a
  piece per day. Its day/clock association follows from the full axis.

  ## 4. Logical requests and explicit selection [# civil-clock-section-7]

  Requests contain exactly [$ operation] and [$ input]. The generic input vocabulary
  is [$ left], [$ right], [$ value], [$ amount], [$ context], [$ policy]; operation arity narrows
  it. Relations require [$ left] and [$ right]. [$ shiftClock] requires [$ value], [$ amount].
  [$ step] and [$ materialize] require [$ value]. All objects are closed.

  Context has only an optional nonempty scalar-string [$ profile]. Policy is closed:
  relations/shiftClock permit only optional [$ profile]; step requires [$ clockForm],
  [$ count]; materialize requires [$ clockForm]; both also permit [$ profile].
  Selectors are [$ hour], [$ minute], [$ second]. Counts are canonical signed whole-number
  text ([$ 0], or optional minus and a nonzero integer), guarded by character count
  before conversion. There is no calendar unit, fractional count or output default.

  Explicit profile selection MUST come from policy or context. Validate both
  supplied configurations, then let policy override context and resolve only the
  effective identifier. Missing selection is [$ invalid_policy], stage 3,
  [$ /input/policy/profile]. A well-formed unavailable effective identifier is
  [$ unsupported_profile] at its selected field. An overridden well-formed unavailable
  identifier need not resolve; malformed overridden configuration still fails.
  Context supplies no counts/selectors. No previous profile/default changes.

  ## 5. Relations [# civil-clock-section-8]

  All outputs are symbols, not Booleans:

  - [$ equal]: exact full-relative-set equality gives [$ equal], otherwise [$ notEqual].
  - [$ compare]: identical sets give [$ equal]; every left member strictly below every
    right member gives [$ before]; reverse gives [$ after]; otherwise [$ indeterminate].
    Coincident extreme bounds are strictly ordered if at least one touching bound
    is excluded. This relation is partial, not a sorting policy.
  - [$ relate]: exactly [$ equal], strict [$ contains], strict [$ containedBy], [$ overlaps]
    for nonempty intersection without containment, or [$ disjoint].

  Use endpoint-aware sweeps, not a Cartesian product or enumeration of members.
  Disjoint sets may interleave and compare as indeterminate. Origin/history and
  authored representation identity are separate from value equality. These labels
  describe positions on the chosen relative axis, not which dated event occurred first.

  ## 6. Shifts, stepping and materialization [# civil-clock-section-9]

  [$ shiftClock] translates every member by the exact finite-decimal amount in
  civil-coordinate seconds. Translate boundaries and retain inclusion flags and
  gaps. Preserve all day carry/borrow, including across repeated operations. A
  zero shift preserves coverage and still pays the prescribed admission/output
  charges. It does not clear history; a marked zero amount propagates history.

  [$ step] selects exactly one complete clock form. Construct the single candidate
  containing the first piece's lower coordinate (whether included or excluded):
  hour/minute round down fields only to build a candidate; second uses that exact
  coordinate, including fraction. Candidate hour/minute is a half-open interval of
  3,600/60 seconds; second is a point. Compare the candidate with the entire input.
  Mismatch is [$ incompatible_operand], stage 6, [$ /input/value/temporal]. The candidate
  does not authorize rounding, widening or searching for other forms.

  Translate a matching form by count × 3,600, 60 or 1 coordinate seconds for its
  selector. Retain the fraction for second forms and all relative days. Count zero
  still validates the complete form and pays source/target accounting. Eligibility
  depends on exact coverage and explicit selection, never origin metadata.

  [$ materialize] uses the same deterministic candidate/matching process, then also
  requires the candidate's starting day to be [$ 0]. A matching last hour/minute can
  have an excluded midnight end on day [$ 1]; it contains no day-1 member and is valid.
  Mismatch or a nonzero starting day is stage-7 [$ unrepresentable_result], null path.
  No derived day is projected away. Emit [$ HH:], [$ HH:MM], or [$ HH:MM:SS] with a
  canonical nonzero fraction, selected explicitly. Return it in the value/history
  envelope. A bare clock component from another day is display, not materialization.

  All successful set results are nonempty canonical sets. Operation failure returns
  no value, partial set, null, Boolean or implicit approximation. Input bundles remain
  available to callers; no operation mutates a caller's semantic bundle.

  ## 7. Result envelopes and metadata [# civil-clock-section-10]

  Success has exactly [$ outcome: "value"], [$ value], [$ metadata]. Arithmetic value is
  a full temporal bundle with required [$ originClockForm] and [$ history]: shifts/steps
  return [$ civilClockSet]; materialization returns [$ civilClock]. Preserve origin.
  History is sticky: preserve the temporal marker and additionally set it when a
  clock amount has [$ approximation-declared]. Never infer it from lexical scale.
  No clamping marker or implicit uncertainty interval is introduced.

  Metadata has [$ evaluation: "exact-for-policy"] and [$ effectivePolicy], containing
  the selected profile and only the applicable explicit selector/count. Relations
  add [$ operandHistory: {left, right}] and [$ operandOrigin: {left, right}], each side
  holding that operand's marker string. Relations' value is exactly
  [$ {category:"symbol", value:label}] and has no temporal history bundle.

  Diagnostic has exactly [$ outcome: "diagnostic"], [$ reason],
  [$ context: {stage, path}], and optional human-readable [$ message]. No [$ value] or
  success metadata. Reasons are [$ invalid_operand], [$ invalid_policy],
  [$ unsupported_operation], [$ unsupported_profile], [$ unsupported_domain],
  [$ incompatible_operand], [$ unrepresentable_result], [$ resource_limit]. Context stage
  is 1–7 and path is a JSON Pointer string or null. Diagnostics never become values
  automatically. Detailed provenance and standard AEON document annotations are deferred.

  ## 8. Portable work and workspace [# civil-clock-section-11]

  Let P be the temporal input's admitted piece count (an authored clock has one),
  and L/R the counts for a relation. A point counts as one piece; interval width
  and day span do not increase that count.

  - Relations: work L+R, no intermediate candidates, one symbolic result.
  - Clock shift: work P, intermediate P, final P (translation preserves components).
  - Materialize: source matching work P+1, intermediate one; final semantic extent
    one even though output is a compact literal.
  - Step: first admit source work P+1 and one reference candidate before matching.
    On match admit total work P+2 and two candidates, then construct the target.
    These totals also apply to zero count. Final target has one piece.

  Matching reference construction needs at most one additional day digit for a
  midnight endpoint. It is bounded by admitted coordinate text plus a constant;
  it performs no decimal exponent alignment and has no numeric-workspace charge.
  Temporary reference endpoints are not supplied/final output capacity claims.
  Never enumerate the days, seconds or fraction grid inside an interval.

  For nonzero [$ shiftClock] amounts use this mandatory conservative decimal-workspace
  recipe, independent of private representations or optimizations. Let p be the
  largest canonical fraction digit count among input endpoints, D the largest
  magnitude digit count among their day fields (zero costs one), c/e the admitted
  normalized nonzero amount, and k = digits(|c|):

  ~~~$ text
  q = max(p, max(0, -e))
  A = k + e + q
  W = max(D + 5 + q, A) + 1
  ~~~$

  [$ D+5] bounds the integer digits of [$ 86,400 × day + clockSeconds]; the last [$ +1]
  reserves addition carry. Admit W against [$ maxWorkspaceDigits], then q against
  [$ maxFractionDigits], before power-of-ten construction, alignment or coordinate
  translation. Check compact integer lengths without expanding padding. Guard work
  then candidates after those checks. This is the portable admission recipe even
  when cancellation could make the final result small; no implementation privately
  changes which requests succeed by selecting another bound.

  For nonzero step count let n be its magnitude digit count and u be the selector's
  integer width (3,600/60/1). After matching and total-work/candidate admission use
  q=p and [$ W=max(D+5+q, n+digits(u)+q)+1]. The multiplication bound is conservative
  and mandatory; it precedes count conversion/product/scaling. Fraction q has
  already been admitted as input. Zero shift amounts/counts have zero numeric
  workspace charge, but all other checks/accounting remain. Do not infer a new
  numerical arithmetic capability or change its existing workspace rules.

  After admission exact arithmetic may use aligned integers. For any resulting
  coordinate x, set d=floor(x/86,400) and r=x−86,400d, so 0≤r<86,400. Render r as
  canonical clock fields/fraction and d as canonical signed day text. This rule
  handles negative fractions correctly and never truncates division toward zero.
  Output day-digit capacity is checked before returning a bundle; do not replace
  it with a fixed host integer range. Intermediate work is bounded by the admitted
  workspace, not by the eventual result byte count alone.

  ## 9. Ordered validation [# civil-clock-section-12]

  Stop at the first reached failure; never return multiple competing diagnostics.

  1. Scalar Unicode, actual logical JSON UTF-8 bytes and per-path container depth
     before deep decoding. Malformed scalar text is stage-1 [$ invalid_operand];
     reached bytes/depth failure is [$ resource_limit], null path.
  2. Strict JSON/duplicate keys, closed request/input structure, operation availability,
     then operation-specific arity. Duplicate keys anywhere fail [$ invalid_operand],
     null path; other failures identify the offending field. Unavailable operations
     use [$ unsupported_operation] at [$ /operation] before operand/configuration work.
  3. Context structure, policy structure/required fields, context profile and policy
     profile syntax, selector/count checks, effective profile selection/availability.
     Count text is guarded before canonicality/conversion. Missing selection and
     malformed configuration use [$ invalid_policy]; reached count allowance uses
     [$ resource_limit]; unavailable selected profile uses [$ unsupported_profile].
  4. Validate operand categories, closed bundle fields and metadata, then descriptors
     and payloads. Relations left then right; shifts temporal value then amount.
     Supported malformed input fails before stage-5 deferred rejection. Use
     [$ invalid_operand] or reached [$ resource_limit] at the relevant field.
  5. Reject recognized deferred domains in operand order with [$ unsupported_domain]
     at the descriptor category field, leaving deferred payload opaque.
  6. Relations guard summed work before relation evaluation. Shift guards numeric
     workspace, generated fraction, work, candidates, then translates. Step guards
     source work/candidate before matching; mismatch is [$ incompatible_operand];
     then total work/candidates, numeric workspace, target construction. Materialize
     guards source work/candidate, then matching. Operation-wide guard paths are null.
  7. Materialization exact-match/day-zero eligibility; produced relative-day digit
     allowance; final semantic-piece allowance; bare materialized text allowance;
     successful compact-envelope UTF-8 allowance before serialization. Reached guards
     use [$ resource_limit], null path; representability is [$ unrepresentable_result].

  Within a closed object, required fields precede unknown keys; unknown decoded keys
  use Unicode scalar order and escaped JSON Pointer tokens. List required fields
  in the shown binding order. At stage 4 require temporal/number descriptor and its
  string category first; for a derived clock require [$ originClockForm], then history;
  check unknown bundle fields; validate history then origin; check descriptor fields
  before payload. Authored bundles use the same supplied-metadata order before defaults.

  For derived sets, nonempty admission precedes the input-length guard, which
  precedes element traversal. Each piece checks kind/required/unknown fields,
  then at/start, end, flags, interval order, cross-piece canonicality. Coordinates
  check required day/time and unknown fields, then day type/text allowance/canonical
  syntax, then clock type/text allowance/grammar, fraction allowance/canonicality,
  intrinsic clock ranges. [$ maxRelativeDayDigits] counts scalar characters after
  removing at most one initial minus, before requiring ASCII digit canonicality;
  plus is not removed. [$ maxTemporalCharacters] counts clock-string Unicode scalars.
  Both coordinates' flags are validated after their coordinates. Authored intrinsic
  clock validity precedes its implicit day/piece capacities, each one.

  Day/fraction/clock failures use the particular coordinate field. A derived input
  piece guard uses [$ .../temporal/pieces]; authored implicit day/piece guard uses
  [$ .../temporal]. Zero/reversed interval uses [$ .../end]; noncanonical neighbors use
  the offending lower coordinate field. A step mismatch uses [$ /input/value/temporal].
  Final output/day/workspace/work/candidate failures use null paths. No first failing
  image coordinate is selected by a private traversal/normalization order.

  For numerical amounts preserve inherited finite/exact-decimal validation: text
  characters before grammar, authored exponent magnitude before normalization even
  for zero, then normalized coefficient/exponent limits. Derived coefficient zero
  requires exponent zero; no repairing noncanonical input. Supplied null history is
  invalid. A malformed supported amount still fails before a deferred temporal
  operand; a deferred descriptor's payload is not decoded as a supported amount.

  Recognized category vocabulary is [$ civilClock], [$ civilClockSet], [$ civilDate],
  [$ civilDateSet], [$ civilDateTime], [$ civilDateTimeSet], [$ finiteNumber], [$ exactNumber],
  [$ exactRational], [$ string], [$ boolean], [$ symbol], [$ positiveInfinity], [$ negativeInfinity],
  [$ nan], [$ explicitNull], [$ explicitAbsence], [$ toggle], [$ hex], [$ radix], [$ encoding],
  [$ separator], [$ sansaAddress], [$ referenceForm], [$ temporal], [$ container]. Only the
  first two are supported temporal descriptors; only finite/exact-decimal tags
  are supported numerical amounts. Others are deferred after containing bundle/
  supplied metadata validation. Unknown tags are malformed. This vocabulary changes
  no frozen predecessor's recognized tags or admitted cases.

  ## 10. Explicit limits and host boundary [# civil-clock-section-13]

  These settings define the snapshot CTS calibration map. They establish neither native defaults nor verified support floors:

  ~~~$ json
  {
    "maxRequestBytes": 65536,
    "maxRequestDepth": 32,
    "maxTemporalCharacters": 1024,
    "maxInputPieces": 512,
    "maxIntermediatePieces": 4096,
    "maxOutputPieces": 512,
    "maxWork": 16384,
    "maxFractionDigits": 256,
    "maxRelativeDayDigits": 256,
    "maxCountCharacters": 32,
    "maxNumericCharacters": 4096,
    "maxCoefficientDigits": 1024,
    "maxExponentMagnitude": 1000000,
    "maxWorkspaceDigits": 4096,
    "maxOutputCharacters": 1024,
    "maxResultBytes": 131072
  }
  ~~~$

  Input pieces are per temporal operand. Temporal text is per supplied clock
  literal/coordinate. Fraction digits include authored trailing zeroes; generated
  fractions are canonical. Relative-day capacity applies to each supplied/final
  coordinate, including excluded endpoints, with magnitude zero costing one digit.
  Amount characters cover the full finite spelling or coefficient+exponent text;
  coefficient digits exclude sign. Exponent magnitude applies before and after
  finite normalization. Output characters count only the bare materialized clock.
  Output pieces count semantic extent, including the one-piece materialized form.
  Result bytes cover the complete successful compact JSON envelope, including all
  metadata. Successful strings are bounded ASCII; size it before rendering large
  strings. No optional message belongs in a success. Diagnostic messages are not
  required, and output-capacity denial MUST never suppress a required diagnostic.

  Use saturating counters before allocation/expansion. All limits are per operation;
  applications manage chains. Trusted limits are known nonnegative safe integers;
  null, arrays, scalars, unknown settings, fractions, Booleans and unsafe numbers
  are harness configuration errors. Omitted known settings may use defaults;
  semantic CTS vectors supply a complete explicit map. Private allocation optimizations
  cannot change the portable admission recipe or excuse rejecting required cases.

  Logical JSON retains duplicate-key and raw numeric token evidence, including
  [$ -0], huge exponent and underflow token spellings inside deferred payloads.
  Native logical JSON admission MUST not fully unfold shared subgraphs before
  byte/depth rejection; cycle handling MUST fail closed. Arbitrary native accessors,
  proxies, custom subclasses, eager key enumeration costs, outer transport/framing,
  process/heap, whole-chain and Cartesian-limit capacities remain documented host
  boundaries. No AST scanner or namespace callback contract is introduced here.

`
